so the sandman really snuck up on me i  legit forgot it was coming out until the  day it dropped it might also be the best  thing dc has put their name on in years  how's it going everybody i'm red muffler  man and once again i've been dragged out  into the content minds for your  enjoyment today we need to talk about  netflix's dc comics vertigos neil  gaiman's the sandman and  oh boy do i have stuff to say about this  show the first being that i'm actually  surprised the show even exists at all  for those that don't know sandman has a  very  troubled history in terms of adaptations  they're attempts to get sandman made  into a movie starting all the way back  in 1991 and then every attempt to get it  adapted ended up in production hell for  all intents and purposes sandman seemed  like a cursed property that would never  get a fully realized adaptation but then  here comes netflix hey neil want to make  an adaptation of your beloved 1990s cult  comic sensation oh  yes  yes that sounds lovely excellent i will  now proceed to cancel bone  again  maybe one day  but yeah getting into the show itself  the first thing i want to touch on here  are some of the differences between the  comic and the adaptation and one of the  first things that comes to my attention  was the general scrubbing of most of the  dc mainstays you see sandman originally  was set somewhat heavy-handedly in the  dc universe in fact sandman himself  started as a forgotten dc hero that neil  wanted to reboot but from the get-go one  of the first storylines of sandman  sawdream referencing the existence of  superman and in another immediately  crossing paths with scot-free and  martian manhunter when dream goes to  visit loose for morningstar in hell in  the comics he's greeted by etrigan a  demon synonymous with the dc universe  created by jack kirby but in the  adaptation the demon is just  some dude and for the most part every  crossover element from the original  comics has mostly been scrubbed with the  exception of static shock so yeah this  was a pleasant surprise for me honestly  seeing jed in the last arc of the show  randomly wearing a static shock t-shirt  granted that surprise quickly went to  the wayside once he realized he's being  abandoned by his mother due to parental  custody issues but it's fine cause then  you cut the jet at serial killer con  watching a full episode of static shock  in the hotel room tv so yeah the show  either has no continuity in the greater  dc universe or we live in a hellscape  where the day-to-day lives of these  superheroes have been flanderized and  produced into saturday morning cartoon  shows oh  i love the boys  but yeah jokes aside i'm pretty happy  with this show not having to deal with  any of the continuity garbage in any  other present universe the show gave  neil and the team a chance to really  stretch their legs with what the sandman  is and that extra freedom shows with how  much fun they got to have with this  adaptation and on that topic the sandman  goes to incredible lengths to pay homage  to the original illustrations found in  the books many of the shots are ripped  almost verbatim and recreated in full  live action one great example being  dream once he finally manages to escape  his glass prison and confronts alex he's  almost fully blacked out with the  exception of his ominous glowing white  eyes i saw that and all i could say to  myself was  why why can't sandman's contemporaries  have this kind of stylistic eye it's  honestly a breath of fresh air from the  drab greys and washed out palettes i've  been assaulted with lately from a  good chunk of dc and mcu films sandman  while it tends to keep a lot of its  shots very dark to match the style of  the comic a lot of scenes also tend to  be lit with very bright splashes of  colored light and this adds a lot of  vibrancy to what would normally just be  a dark and muddy mess and it really  helps the show stand apart visually  despite some spotty cgi  all those gargoyles but to my next point  we move on to the sandman goes to hell  and this scene differs pretty hard from  the one we got in the comics the setup  is roughly the same dream is in search  of his helmet and after learning it's  been traded to a demon he journeys to  hell to have an audience with lucifer  morningstar lucifer brings forward the  demon quran's on who has the helmet  dream and coronzon must now do battle in  order to decide who will get the helmet  in the netflix adaptation each side  names a champion dream naming himself  and koronzon naming lucifer to be his  champion cause uh yeah that's fair what  proceeds is a psychic battle where dream  and lucifer are challenging their wits  world views and realities in a grandiose  struggle that sees dream on the brink of  death well pat and oswald cries to the  heavens asking why  only for dream to fight back with the  power of hope winning against lucifer  and taking back the helmet in what i can  only describe as a emotional spectacle  it was super cool oh it was so cool but  then you have the comic which sees  korrons on not being a little [ __ ] and  actually fighting his own battles but we  don't get a big emotional fight with big  epic music no no no no no no it's  actually an improv stage show the entire  scene is really playful chronzone  appears with a suit and the two start  throwing reality bending soliloquy  towards one another in front of a live  studio audience i'd expect this type of  at a burlesque show not hell and then to  top it all off dream is just casually  dropping bars dressed up like a blues  brother and he is demolishing this demon  jobber without a second thought there's  no struggle this dude is just dying if  anything i'm actually kind of sad we  didn't get this version of the scene it  shows off dream in a more playful light  where even if he's deadpan you can tell  he's still very quirky and it's kind of  a shame granted we still get to see a  big part of him being a a quirky little  dork i love dream he's  he's a huge dork but then we have what i  think is the best part of sandman and  that is john d the antagonist of the  first arc and for those that don't know  john in the comics initially started off  as a super villain named dr destiny hey  yo that name goes kinda hard though he'd  come into possession of dreams ruby  after dream was forcefully abducted and  proceeded to you said ruby to wreak  absolute havoc on the planet but the  more he used the ruby the more it wore  down on his sanity eventually he ended  up being confined to arkham asylum  however he escapes and using the ruby  once again wreaks havoc until he's  finally put away by the combined efforts  of dream and the jla in the comic john d  comes off as someone who may have been  good at one point but has lost all  meaning in pretty much anything that  they do because they're uh  crazy  john in the show however has a certain  warmth it shows in almost every aspect  of his character from his mother  sacrificing her life to make sure that  he's safe as he's crying over her dead  body  begging her to stop jon  warning his victims  not to attack him  lest they hurt themselves  you can tell by speaking with john that  he's not well  but  he still shows a shred of humanity no  matter how dark his actions may be  and the best example i feel is comparing  how he handles rosemary in the comics  versus the show for those that don't  know rosemary was the person that  happened to pick up john while he was  hitchhiking back to his house and uh  very unfortunate for her rosemary in the  comics remains oblivious to jon's true  nature throughout her driving him around  she speaks with john who vents to her  about his problems and the two seem to  get close to one another then once they  arrive jon just  casually shoots rosemary and takes the  ruby of dream any sort of remorse was  left at the door a long long time ago  but then we get the same scene in the  show rosemary knows that jon is not well  she's seen him kill someone and for all  intents and purposes she's jon's hostage  she drives john to his old house for him  to pick up the ruby  just like in the comic  at this point i fully expected john to  blow her brains out and move on being  this full-on unforgivable villain he  tells her that good people tend to not  be able to protect themselves and  he gives her the amulet of protection  that his mother gave to him to make sure  that the person who helped him despite  fearing for her life was going to live  the rest of her life in safety this  trend continues in the episode 24 7. in  the comics jon uses the ruby to  manipulate people at a local diner  making their lives hell for no other  reason other than it's just something  entertaining for him but in the show  jon has this childlike obsession  with the truth he wants to live in a  world where lies are something that  simply do not exist and everyone can  truly connect with one another jon  idolizes this perfect place where people  don't have to suffer the deceit that he  had to go through that ruined his entire  life and while this leads the tragic  slaughter of the people in the diner  jon was coming from a place of wanting  to do what he felt was right he wants to  live in a world of truth even if that  truth is ugly even if that truth hurts  people and that childlike obsession  makes him a far more interesting villain  than his original appearance in the  comics  it feels  grounded sincere and i think that's why  i like the sandman as much as i do this  is a quote in the final episode that  perfectly encapsulates my feelings on  this show humanity is far more vivid to  us  than any marvels of power sometimes to  tell a compelling story it's not about  displaying incredible feats of strength  or power it's not about how good your  cgi gargoyles are how clean your  penciling is in a comic or how much  budget you have for special effects  sometimes it's enough to sit down and  tell the story of what it means to be a  human to put pen to paper and let those  sincere thoughts out and  that's all i have to say about sandman  it's great i can't recommend it enough  please watch it and feel free to drop  your thoughts on the sandman in the  comments below i want to thank rob once  again for letting me gush about this  cool little show and if you like what we  do feel free to drop a like and  subscribe and if you like me i'm on  twitch twitter and youtube at red  muffler mad and uh yeah have a good one  everybody take it easy
 

